.SH DESCRIPTION
OmaWord reads the Bible offline, with highlights, tags, Markdown notes, and a
study Desk, following the Omarchy theme. Without arguments it starts, or
brings forward the window you were reading; with a passage it opens it in a
new window.
.PP
Commands other than
.B open
and
.B lookup
run without a window and never move your reading place; those two go to the
running OmaWord, which starts if it is not running yet. Of the others, only
.BR "translations install" ,
.BR remove ,
and
.B import
change anything: the library, as Versions does, which a running OmaWord's
windows follow.
.PP
Some commands come in groups named for what they work on, such as
.BR "translations list" ;
a group's name alone runs its first command.
.SH PASSAGES
Full or compact references, as the passage palette reads them:
.IR "John 3:16" ,
.IR jn316 ,
.IR 1co13 ,
.IR "rev 3 14-22" ,
.I ps23
(a whole chapter),
.IR "Genesis 1:1-2:3" .
Unquoted words that make one passage together count as one
.RB ( "omaword quote john 3:16" ).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI open " passage"
Opens the passage in a new window;
.B \-\-here
opens it in the window you were reading. A translation other than that
window's switches it.
.TP
.BI lookup " \fR[\fPtext\fR]\fP"
Opens a small window on the workspace you are on, with
.I text
typed into it, if given: type a passage,
.B enter
shows it,
.B y
copies it
.RB ( "shift Y"
the plain text),
.B enter
opens it in the window you were reading, and
.B esc
closes it. With more than one version installed,
.B tab
and
.B shift tab
read it in another, which
.B enter
then opens. It can be resized and opens at its last size. Suits a key
binding; a window rule for its title,
.RI \(lq "Look up \[pc] OmaWord" \(rq,
can float and center it and turn off its animation
.RB ( "float = true, center = true, no_anim = true" ).
.TP
.BI read " passage..."
Prints each passage as OmaWord's reader shows it, under its reference and
translation: section headings, paragraphs, poem lines indented by their level,
verse numbers, and a numbered marker where a footnote is;
.B \-\-footnotes
prints the notes after the passage. Verse numbers, headings, and the words of
Jesus in red follow OmaWord's settings. Lines wrap to the terminal's width,
piped into a pager too.
.BR \-\-color " auto|always|never"
bolds titles and headings and colors the words of Jesus;
.B \-\-json
prints a JSON array with each verse's heading, paragraph, poem levels,
footnotes, and words of Jesus. Passages come from standard input as for
.BR quote .
.TP
.BI quote " passage..."
Prints each passage as OmaWord copies it: the text in quotation marks, a
number before each verse, and the reference after an em dash, a blank line
between passages.
.B \-\-all
quotes each passage in every installed translation, the one you read first,
each reference followed by the translation's abbreviation; one a translation
lacks is noted, and only one none has fails.
.B \-\-plain
prints the text alone;
.B \-\-json
a JSON array with each passage's verses. Given
.BR \- ,
or no passage while input is piped in, it reads passages from standard input,
one per line.
.TP
.BI refs " passage..."
Prints what OmaWord's See also gives for each passage: the passages tagged
alike (sharing its tags, or those of marks over it) in every translation, then
OpenBible.info's cross-references, most voted first, each with its opening
words. See also follows verses, not whole chapters.
.B \-\-quote
prints each passage in full, as a quotation;
.BI \-\-limit " n"
prints at most
.I n
a section (0: all; the default is 20 on a terminal and all otherwise);
.B \-\-json
prints a JSON array with each passage's text. The exit status is 1 when a
passage has neither.
.TP
.BI search " words..."
Searches as OmaWord does: every word in one verse, partial words included; a
"quoted phrase" may run across verses. Words in slashes, such as
.BR '/lov(e|ed)/' ,
are a regular expression matched within one verse. Case is ignored unless a
word (or the expression) has a capital letter
.RB ( \ec
ignores it,
.B \eC
keeps it). Matches print in Bible order.
.B \-\-all
searches every installed translation, the one you read first;
.BI \-\-in " part"
searches only the Old or New Testament
.RB ( ot ", " nt ),
the books after them
.RB ( apocrypha ),
or a book, such as
.B John
or
.BR jn ,
and may be repeated;
.BI \-\-limit " n"
prints at most
.I n
(0: all; the default is 20 on a terminal and all otherwise);
.BR \-\-color " auto|always|never"
emphasizes matched words;
.B \-\-json
prints a JSON array.
.TP
.BI link " passage..."
Prints an
.B omaword:
link to each passage.
.TP
.B books \fR[\fPlist\fR]\fP
Lists the translation's books, in its order, with how many chapters each has.
.TP
.BI "books outline" " book\fR|\fPchapter"
Prints the section headings of a book, or of one of its chapters, as the
whole-book view's outline has them: each chapter, then its headings after the
verses they open.
.TP
.B marks \fR[\fPlist\fR] [\fIwords\fR...]\fP
Lists your marks (each passage you highlighted, wrote a note on, or tagged) in
the translation you read, in Bible order: its reference and translation,
whether it is highlighted, and its tags, then its note on one line, or its
opening words without one. As in OmaWord's Marks, each word must match a
mark's reference, translation, note, tags, or text, ignoring case, and
.BI # name
matches a tag starting with
.IR name ,
as
.BI \-\-tag " name"
does (repeat it to need more tags).
.B \-\-notes
and
.B \-\-highlights
keep only the marks with a note or a highlight;
.B \-\-all
lists every installed translation's. The exit status is 1 when nothing
matches.
.TP
.BI "marks show" " passage"
Prints the marks on a passage or within it, as
.B marks list
does but with each note whole, as you wrote it.
.TP
.B marks export
Prints your marks as Markdown, as OmaWord's Export writes them: a section for
each translation, then each mark's reference, highlight, tags, and note.
.BI \-o " file"
writes them to a file instead, readable by you alone.
.TP
.BI "marks backup" " file\fR|\fP\-"
Writes the backup OmaWord's Back up writes, which its Restore reads: every
translation's marks and reading place, your tags, and the Desk, in a file
readable by you alone;
.B \-
prints it instead. Neither command writes over OmaWord's own databases.
.TP
.B tags \fR[\fPlist\fR]\fP
Lists your tags by name, each with how many passages it marks.
.TP
.B desk \fR[\fPshow\fR]\fP
Prints the Desk, the Markdown page every window shares.
.TP
.B history \fR[\fPlist\fR]\fP
Lists the passages you visited, newest first, each after when you did.
.TP
.B ribbon \fR[\fPshow\fR]\fP
Prints the passage where the ribbon lies; the exit status is 1 when it lies
nowhere.
.TP
.B translations \fR[\fPlist\fR]\fP
Lists the installed translations;
.B *
marks the one you read.
.B \-\-names
prints the abbreviations alone.
.RB ( "omaword versions"
is the same.)
.TP
.BI "translations show" " \fR[\fPname\fR]\fP"
Prints what OmaWord knows of an installed translation, by default the one you
read: its name, abbreviation, ID, language, and contents, AO's revision of it,
and its rights as its source gives them, which About & Attribution shows too.
.TP
.BI "translations install" " id..."
Downloads each translation from AO Lab, named by its ID or abbreviation in the
catalog, checks it, and installs it, or replaces the installed copy, keeping
your marks. Progress shows on a terminal; the exit status is 1 when one fails.
.TP
.BI "translations remove" " name..."
Removes each installed translation. Your marks in it stay for when you install
it again, unless
.B \-\-delete\-marks
deletes them too; your tags and the Desk stay either way.
.TP
.BI "translations import" " file"
Installs a translation, or replaces the installed copy keeping your marks, from
a file of AO standard complete JSON.
.RB ( "omaword import"
is the same.) Installing, removing, and importing leave the translation you
read the one you read, unless none is installed yet.
.TP
.B catalog \fR[\fPlist\fR] [\fIwords\fR...]\fP
Lists the translations in AO Lab's catalog, as last refreshed (else as
OmaWord came with it), in English by default: each one's abbreviation, name,
and ID, and whether it is installed. Each word must match a translation's
name, English name, ID, or abbreviation, ignoring case.
.BI \-\-language " code"
lists another language's, by its ISO 639-3 code;
.B \-\-all\-languages
lists every language's. The exit status is 1 when none matches.
.TP
.BI "catalog show" " id"
Prints what AO Lab's catalog says of a translation, named by its ID or
abbreviation: its name, language, and contents, whether it is installed, and
its terms and permissions, as Details & Attribution shows them.
.TP
.B catalog refresh
Downloads AO Lab's current catalog, which
.B catalog
reads from then on, as do the OmaWord windows that open after it.
.TP
.BI completion " shell"
Prints the completion script for
.BR bash ,
.BR zsh ,
or
.BR fish .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-translation " \fIname\fP"
The translation to use: its abbreviation (NET) or ID (eng_net), in any case.
By default, the one you read.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Shows help; after a command, that command's.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Shows OmaWord's version.
.SH LINKS
.B omaword:John%203:16
and
.B omaword:jn316?translation=KJV
open the passage in the window you were reading;
.B omaword:
alone brings that window forward. The desktop entry registers
the scheme, so
.BR xdg-open (1)
and browsers hand such links to OmaWord.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set,
.BR read ,
.BR refs ,
and
.B search
color nothing unless asked with
.BR "\-\-color always" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/omaword/
Translations, marks, the Desk, History, and the ribbon.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/omaword/search-index.sqlite
The search index, rebuilt whenever it is missing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when it fails (an unknown passage or translation, no match,
nothing installed); 2 for a usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
omaword read ps23 \-\-footnotes
omaword quote jn316
omaword quote ps23 \-t KJV \-\-plain | wl\-copy
omaword search '"living water"' \-\-all
omaword search '/lov(e|ed) the world/'
omaword search shepherd \-\-in ot \-\-in John
omaword open "Romans 8" \-\-here
omaword translations install BSB
xdg\-open "$(omaword link jn316)"
.fi
